  "formula": "e_W(u) = ||W^T u||^2 / ||W||_F^2. Write v1 = cos(t) r + sin(t) q with q unit, q perp r. Then e_W(v1) = cos^2(t) e_W(r) + sin^2(t) e_W(q) + 2 cos(t) sin(t) <W^T r, W^T q> / ||W||_F^2. Cauchy-Schwarz on the last two terms gives |e_W(v1) - cos^2(t) e_W(r)| <= sin^2(t) e_max + 2 |cos(t) sin(t)| sqrt(e_W(r) e_max), where e_max = lambda_max(W W^T)/||W||_F^2 <= 1. min_m is 1-Lipschitz, so the same bound carries to the minimum over matrices, hence to W05 = log10 min_m e_{W_m}(v1).",
